Editor Plugin Development Guide for coole
=========================================

This document describes how to write an editor plugin for coole: a
module that registers with the editor and adds commands, menu entries
and key handling of its own.

The authoritative declarations live in:

- lib/editor-plugin.h        the plugin API
- lib/editor-plugin.c        the registry and the loader

This guide reflects the current API only.  When in doubt, the header
is the source of truth.

Scripts written in Lua are a different mechanism: they are run by the
Lua runtime plugin and need no compiler.  See doc/LUA_PLUGINS.md and
doc/LUA_API_REFERENCE.md.


1. General Rules
----------------

- Keep the editor core plugin-agnostic.
  Core must not branch on a plugin's name; expose a capability flag or
  callback instead.
- Use the host callbacks (mc_editor_host_t) rather than reaching into
  editor internals where a callback exists.
- Return explicit status codes:
  MC_EPR_OK / MC_EPR_FAILED / MC_EPR_NOT_SUPPORTED.
- Keep activate() for the main action and configure() for the settings
  dialog; do not overload activate() with a configuration dialog.


2. In-tree Plugins
------------------

The plugins that come with coole are built into the program (see
src/meson.build, which compiles them into the internal library):

  src/editor-plugins/ctags/      self-registered menu entries, named
                                 actions, dynamic shortcut resolver
  src/editor-plugins/spell/      activate + configure + query_state +
                                 handle_key
  src/editor-plugins/etags/      handle_action only

src/editor-plugins/builtin-plugins.c (compiled as part of
src/editor/editcmd.c) holds their descriptors (and that
of the "scripts" entry) and registers them with
mc_editor_plugin_add() from editor_plugins_register_all().

The help of a plugin (ctags.md, etags.md, spell.md) is installed into
$(pkgdatadir)/help next to the help of the program.


3. Lifecycle and Location
-------------------------

When the editor starts, it first registers the built-in plugins and
then calls mc_editor_plugins_load(), which scans two directories:

    $(libdir)/coole/editor-plugins          (MC_EDITOR_PLUGINS_DIR)
    ~/.local/lib/coole/editor-plugins

The system directory is set at build time:

    meson setup build -Deditor_plugins_dir=DIR

Every file directly in these directories whose name ends in .so (or
.dylib, .bundle, .dll) is opened with g_module_open(); there are no
subdirectories.  The loader resolves the symbol
"mc_editor_plugin_register" (MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_ENTRY), calls it, and
passes the descriptor it returns to mc_editor_plugin_add().  A module
whose descriptor is accepted is made resident.  Loading needs GModule;
a build without it has the built-in plugins only.

mc_editor_plugin_add() rejects a descriptor

- whose api_version differs from MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_API_VERSION
  (currently 5), with a message on stderr;
- that has no name, no open() or no close(), with a message on stderr;
- whose name was disabled in Options -> Manage plugins;
- whose name is already registered.  The built-in plugins are
  registered first, so a module cannot replace one of them.

For every editor dialog, the editor calls open() of each registered
plugin that is not disabled.  A plugin whose open() returns NULL gets
no instance in that dialog.  close() receives the value open()
returned.

Options -> Manage plugins switches plugins on and off; the state is
kept in ~/.config/coole/plugins.ini, section [DisabledPlugins], one key
"editor/<name>" per disabled plugin.  Options -> Plugin info lists the
registered plugins and the callbacks each one has.


4. API Entry Point
------------------

A plugin module must export:

    const mc_editor_plugin_t *mc_editor_plugin_register (void);

The descriptor minimum:

- api_version = MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_API_VERSION
- a unique name
- display_name (the menu label; name is used when it is NULL)
- the required callbacks open and close

    void *(*open) (mc_editor_host_t *host, void *editor_dialog);
    void (*close) (void *plugin_data);


5. Capability Flags (mc_ep_flags_t)
-----------------------------------

  MC_EPF_NONE
  MC_EPF_HAS_MENU            the plugin has an entry of its own in the
                             Plugins menu.  The entry is shown only when
                             the plugin also has activate()


6. Host Services (mc_editor_host_t)
-----------------------------------

What the editor provides to the plugin, passed to open():

  redraw(host)
                             redraw the editor window
  message(host, flags, title, text)
                             modal message box
  get_cursor_word(host, edit)
                             word under cursor; NULL if not on a word.
                             Caller frees with g_free()
  get_current_file(host, edit)
                             absolute path of the open file or NULL;
                             caller frees
  get_cursor_line(host, edit)
                             1-based line number; 0 if unavailable
  jump_to(host, edit, file, line)
                             open file at line; pushes the current
                             position onto the navigation stack
  insert_text(host, edit, text, remove_before)
                             insert at cursor after deleting
                             remove_before bytes backwards

Fields:

  host_data                  opaque, owned by the editor core

The edit and editor_dialog arguments are opaque to the plugin.


7. Plugin Callbacks
-------------------

All are optional except open and close.

  activate(plugin_data, edit)
                             the primary action, run when the plugin's
                             entry in the Plugins menu is picked
  configure(plugin_data, edit)
                             open the settings dialog
  handle_action(plugin_data, command, edit)
                             called for the plugin's own Plugins menu
                             command when it has no activate(), and
                             offered every editor command (CK_*) the
                             editor executes
  query_state(plugin_data, edit, &state)
                             fill mc_ep_state_t; checked before
                             activate() from the Plugins menu
  handle_key(plugin_data, key, edit)
                             raw key interception.  Called for every
                             key after the macro hotkeys and before the
                             keymap is consulted;
                             return MC_EPR_OK to consume the key
  handle_event(plugin_data, edit, event_id, payload)
  on_file_open(plugin_data, edit)
  on_file_close(plugin_data, edit)
                             declared in the header; the editor core
                             does not call these yet (Plugin info lists
                             them as capabilities)

State contract (mc_ep_state_t):

  available                  plugin can operate in the current
                             environment (binaries present, etc.)
  enabled                    action is currently usable
  reason                     optional explanation shown to the user
                             when the action is refused

Event ids reserved for handle_event():

  MC_EP_EVENT_FILE_SAVED      file written; payload = const char *path
  MC_EP_EVENT_FILE_RENAMED    Save As completed; payload = new path
  MC_EP_EVENT_FOCUS_IN        editor window got focus
  MC_EP_EVENT_FOCUS_OUT       editor window lost focus


8. Actions and Menu Entries
---------------------------

A plugin exposes named actions through:

    const mc_ep_action_t *actions;
    int action_count;

Each entry has a label and a direct callback:

    mc_ep_result_t (*callback) (void *plugin_data, void *edit);

The callback is invoked directly when the user picks the menu item;
activate / handle_action are not used for it.  A plugin has at most
MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_ACTIONS_MAX (256) actions.

Top-level menu integration:

    const mc_ep_cmd_menu_entry_t *cmd_menu_entries;
    int cmd_menu_entry_count;

Each entry sets:

    menu_name        MC_EP_MENU_COMMAND or MC_EP_MENU_NAVIGATE
    label            menu text (with & accelerator, translated with
                     gettext); NULL = separator
    action_index     index into actions[]
    shortcut         display string for the shortcut column; NULL =
                     omit.  Overridden at menu-build time if the plugin
                     defines get_menu_shortcut().

Optional dynamic shortcut resolver:

    char *(*get_menu_shortcut) (int action_index);

Returns a newly-allocated string (caller frees with g_free) or NULL to
fall back to the static shortcut text.

Command ids: the Plugins menu entry of the plugin at position N of the
registry is MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_CMD_BASE + N; action A of that plugin is
MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_ACTION_BASE + N * MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_ACTIONS_MAX + A.


9. Registry
-----------

  mc_editor_plugin_add(plugin)
  mc_editor_plugin_list()
  mc_editor_plugin_find_by_name(name)


10. Minimal Skeleton
--------------------

A plugin that registers and reacts to its Plugins menu entry (no extra
menus, no named actions):

    #include <config.h>

    #include "lib/global.h"
    #include "lib/editor-plugin.h"

    static void *
    my_open (mc_editor_host_t *host, void *editor_dialog)
    {
        (void) editor_dialog;
        return host;  /* any non-NULL opaque handle */
    }

    static void
    my_close (void *plugin_data)
    {
        (void) plugin_data;
    }

    static mc_ep_result_t
    my_activate (void *plugin_data, void *edit)
    {
        mc_editor_host_t *host = (mc_editor_host_t *) plugin_data;
        (void) edit;
        if (host->message != NULL)
            host->message (host, 0, "My Plugin", "Hello from the editor.");
        return MC_EPR_OK;
    }

    static const mc_editor_plugin_t my_plugin = {
        .api_version  = MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_API_VERSION,
        .name         = "myedit",
        .display_name = "My Editor Plugin",
        .flags        = MC_EPF_HAS_MENU,

        .open         = my_open,
        .close        = my_close,
        .activate     = my_activate,
    };

    const mc_editor_plugin_t *
    mc_editor_plugin_register (void)
    {
        return &my_plugin;
    }

The headers come from the source tree, and config.h from a configured
build tree of the same version.  With the source in $SRC and the build
tree in $BUILD, a module can be compiled like this:

    cc -shared -fPIC -I"$BUILD" -I"$SRC" \
        $(pkg-config --cflags glib-2.0) \
        -o myedit.so myedit.c

The program is linked with -export-dynamic when GModule is available,
so the module can use the functions of the editor without linking
against them.  Copy myedit.so into ~/.local/lib/coole/editor-plugins
and start coole again: the plugin appears in the Plugins menu, and
selecting it calls my_activate().

The descriptor is checked against MC_EDITOR_PLUGIN_API_VERSION, and a
module built against headers of another API version is not loaded.
Rebuild plugins when coole is upgraded.


11. Testing Checklist
---------------------

- the plugin appears in the Plugins menu, and in Options -> Plugin info
- activate() runs the primary action
- configure() opens settings only
- query_state() reflects backend / runtime availability
- self-registered menu entries land in the right top-level menu
- missing dependencies are reported via a clear user message
- switching the plugin off in Options -> Manage plugins keeps it out
  after a restart


12. Troubleshooting
-------------------

- Plugin not loaded:
  - verify the module is installed directly in one of the plugin
    directories, with a .so suffix
  - verify the exported symbol name (mc_editor_plugin_register)
  - verify api_version matches the header constant
  - run coole from a terminal and read what the loader writes to
    stderr
  - check that the plugin is not disabled in Options -> Manage plugins
  - check that no other plugin, built-in or not, has the same name

- Plugin listed but does nothing:
  - check that the required callbacks are non-NULL
  - confirm correct return codes (NOT_SUPPORTED vs FAILED)
  - for a Plugins menu entry, set MC_EPF_HAS_MENU and provide
    activate()

- Key does not trigger:
  - handle_key() sees the raw key code before the keymap; compare
    against the key code, not a CK_* command
